WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Memory-constrained security work depends on measurement.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:16.680
Estimates are necessary during design, but actual behavior can change with compiler settings, library versions, enabled features, logging levels, alignment, optimization, and target hardware.

3
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.660
Teams need a way to compare the expected memory budget with what the program really uses.

4
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.100
Stack high-water marks help reveal how much stack was used during tests.

5
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:36.920
They are most useful when tests cover valid input, invalid input, error handling, logging, callbacks, and target-specific features.

6
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.940
A shallow test path can make the stack look safer than it is.

7
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:50.020
Heap and allocation tracking help teams understand allocation size, lifetime, peak use, failure behavior, and fragmentation patterns.

8
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:58.440
Even simple counters can show whether memory use grows over time, whether cleanup paths release owned resources, and whether long-running behavior matches the design.

9
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:08.080
Boundary tests should cover minimum, maximum, just-below-maximum, just-above-maximum, empty, truncated, and malformed input.

10
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:14.020
Full-buffer tests should exercise the exact moment when a buffer can no longer accept more data.

11
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.840
These tests make limits real instead of aspirational.

12
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.480
Allocation-failure tests are especially valuable.

13
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:29.940
Force each allocation point to fail and confirm that the program preserves safe state, releases owned resources, and reports a clear result.

14
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:35.400
The test should also confirm that diagnostics do not allocate excessive memory in the failure path.

15
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:42.480
Static analysis, compiler warnings, sanitizers where available, and code review all support the process.

16
00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:50.340
They can find unchecked results, suspicious conversions, out-of-bounds patterns, lifetime issues, and missed error handling.

17
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.880
Tools help, but they do not replace explicit limits and reviewed design assumptions.

18
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.280
Validate on realistic target builds.

19
00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:10.260
A host build with extra memory and different libraries may be useful during development, but constrained behavior must be confirmed with the relevant build profile, configuration, and target assumptions.

20
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.760
The closer the test is to release behavior, the more useful the result.
